#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101763} :activity/id #uuid "0dbeb966-d2d7-56fc-87f7-9f532b108178" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050912 :topic/id #uuid "70abc32f-7c95-5eaa-bc37-2779f513c439" :topic/math-academy-id 1174} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101763 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101920 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we have an alternating series\n\n$$\nS = \\sum_{n =1}^\\infty b_n\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nb_n = (-1)^n a_n\n$$\n\nor\n\n$$\nb_n = (-1)^{n+1} a_n\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\na_n \\geq 0\n$$\n\nLet's assume that the above series is convergent by the alternating series test.\n\nThe $N$th partial sum of this series is\n\n$$\ns_n = \\sum_{n =1}^N b_n\n$$\n\nHow good an estimate of $S$ is the $n$th partial sum of $S$?\n\nThe **alternating series error bound** states that, for a convergent alternating series,\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}S- s_n & < & b_{n+1}\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the magnitude of the error of the $n$th partial sum is less than the magnitude of the $(n+1)$th term." :tutorial/id #uuid "d1ab9f29-4499-5933-a4db-cf6fd5292dd6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1260 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "cc3fd837-dffb-53ad-8a81-f754a99bdf38" :step/math-academy-id 2473 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101764}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101764 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100924 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100772 :question/id #uuid "dfa7cfd6-08dd-507f-aa3a-61beacbc36ca" :question/math-academy-id "e-721"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c1ad1fe5-c690-54ab-a172-fdc69668d60d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050417}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101191} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101467} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536809} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540035} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540077} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575557} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575573}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Alternating Series Error Bound for a Given Partial Sum"} :step/id #uuid "a6217776-80ac-51aa-9ea0-f5fce39fdb15" :step/math-academy-id 2474 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101765}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101765 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100925 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100774 :question/id #uuid "193d6ccc-ffa5-5f36-8ed1-084dea29448f" :question/math-academy-id "e-723"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "be83d82e-531e-52bf-bacb-b621694ce40c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048159}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101186} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101464} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540079} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540086} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575574} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575591} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575625}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Values of N Such That the Error in the Nth Partial Sum Is Smaller Than a Given Number"} :step/id #uuid "05d18ce2-c320-5098-8bb6-ce9ee0b599e9" :step/math-academy-id 2480}] :activity/title "The Alternating Series Error Bound" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186101041}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046645} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540177} :activity/id #uuid "2a564e61-70d4-550a-89c2-4db5bd9ae08b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050912 :topic/id #uuid "70abc32f-7c95-5eaa-bc37-2779f513c439" :topic/math-academy-id 1174} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540131 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540086 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0749bd24-a594-5cc9-83a7-36f58ac8d74e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{2}^{i}\\ge 50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539491} {:answer/id #uuid "14c36eeb-9d6a-50b7-bad3-9d44cf5ab909" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{2}^{i}\\ge 98" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539519} {:answer/id #uuid "390e483e-d568-5e74-bc98-5844f88422f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{2}^{i}\\ge 48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539586} {:answer/id #uuid "4388f0fe-c065-54c2-b8d2-e04e3c15d306" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{2}^{i}\\ge 100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539605} {:answer/id #uuid "8b29d420-50c8-516b-bbbf-e812563a039e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{2}^{i}\\ge 52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539712}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539491} :answer-field/id #uuid "94f7bcdd-1a29-50a3-b5ca-fac98fc4f9d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539972}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "bb487298-b519-5edb-8e52-6ad72f9356fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-104031" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\nS={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{2}^{n}}\n$$\n\nand that ${s}_{i}$ is the $i$th partial sum of $S,$ use the alternating series error bound to determine an inequality that gives the values of $i$ such that $|S-{s}_{i}|<0.01.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let ${a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{{2}^{n}}$ and ${b}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{2}^{n}}.$ Then ${a}_{n}$ is decreasing for $n\\ge 1$ and ${a}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ So, the series is convergent by the alternating series test. \n\nBecause our series is a convergent alternating series, the alternating series error bound applies. In particular, for the $i$th partial sum, it states that\n\n$$\n|S-{s}_{i}|<|{b}_{i+1}|.\n$$\n\nSo, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{b}_{i+1}|=|\\frac{(-1)^{i+1}}{{2}^{i+1}}| & \\le 0.01 \\\\ \\frac{1}{{2}^{i+1}} & \\le \\frac{1}{100} \\\\ 100 & \\le {2}^{i+1} \\\\ 100 & \\le 2\\cdot {2}^{i} \\\\ {2}^{i} & \\ge 50.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3d748b43-7f4a-55a5-b9f2-b2b1e447b3e8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540139}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540139 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540035 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42b4363e-f490-5a92-947a-8650b1bc2add" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{e^{9}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539602}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539602} :answer-field/id #uuid "49c7b2b3-00cb-51d6-bf65-4c63b25afc6e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539953}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "26d0f888-4d20-5d31-b520-f4c4f17a1d7e" :question/math-academy-id "q-352750" :question/problem "Suppose that\n\n$$\nS={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{(-1)}^{n}}{{e}^{n+2}}\n$$\n\nand that ${s}_{6}$ is the sixth partial sum of $S.$ According to the alternating series error bound, $|S-{s}_{6}|<k$ for some real number $k.$\n\nFind the value of $k.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$k=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{{e}^{n+2}}$ and ${b}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{e}^{n+2}}.$ Then ${a}_{n}$ is positive and decreasing for $n\\ge 1,$ and ${a}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ So, the series is convergent by the alternating series test.\n\nBecause our series is a convergent alternating series, the alternating series error bound applies. In particular, for the $6$th partial sum, it states that\n\n$$\n|S-{s}_{6}|<|{b}_{7}|=|\\frac{{(-1)}^{7}}{{e}^{7+2}}|=\\frac{1}{{e}^{9}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=\\frac{1}{{e}^{9}}.$"} :step/id #uuid "52608748-4925-5f07-89c8-df6f306d09b8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540174}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540154 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540079 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1347e9be-27c7-561f-9660-bf0eb27ab9f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{i}^{2}+2i\\ge 100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539517} {:answer/id #uuid "22838a7c-5f32-5a55-9a8a-210f19e97ab1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{i}^{2}+4i\\ge 10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539546} {:answer/id #uuid "69d0a8d6-bd8f-5e35-9b5d-e6a76b35685c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{i}^{2}+4i\\ge 98" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539655} {:answer/id #uuid "8bc70d2a-9194-5400-ae1f-803748c50b7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{i}^{2}+2i\\ge 8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539715} {:answer/id #uuid "f10e5377-afe1-5456-b9e8-239ed8651fc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{i}^{2}+2i\\ge 10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539883}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539883} :answer-field/id #uuid "39823874-955e-5f49-9b30-1ee04d308856" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539941}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "aa9e57c4-993f-58af-8f8c-2c21d29224b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-83612" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\nS={\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}-1}\n$$\n\nand that ${s}_{i}$ is the $i$th partial sum of $S,$ use the alternating series error bound to determine an inequality that gives the values of $i$ such that $|S-{s}_{i}|<0.1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let ${a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}-1}$ and ${b}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}-1}.$ Then ${a}_{n}$ is decreasing for $n\\ge 2$ and ${a}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ So, the series is convergent by the alternating series test. \n\nBecause our series is a convergent alternating series, the alternating series error bound applies. In particular, for the $i$th partial sum, it states that\n\n$$\n|S-{s}_{i}|<|{b}_{i+1}|.\n$$\n\nSo, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{b}_{i+1}|=|\\frac{(-1)^{i+1}}{(i+1)^{2}-1}| & \\le 0.1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{{i}^{2}+2i+1-1} & \\le \\frac{1}{10} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{i}^{2}+2i} & \\le \\frac{1}{10} \\\\ {i}^{2}+2i & \\ge 10.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "82473a92-9dc4-5f79-bf3f-843f63c36ff6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540131}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540174 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540077 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e740dac2-bdf9-576f-b582-e4da8c53bb96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{315}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539871}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539871} :answer-field/id #uuid "aafd742f-ff73-5b71-bc91-fdad1d0edbed" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539980}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a26aabae-4ded-5132-8e5c-c91af894cefb" :question/math-academy-id "q-352755" :question/problem "Suppose that\n\n$$\nS={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{(-2)}^{n}}{(n+1)!}\n$$\n\nand that ${s}_{5}$ is the fifth partial sum of $S.$ According to the alternating series error bound, $|S-{s}_{5}|<k$ for some real number $k.$ Find the value of $k.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$k=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{2}^{n}}{(n+1)!}$ and ${b}_{n}=\\frac{{(-2)}^{n}}{(n+1)!}.$ Then ${a}_{n}$ is positive and decreasing for $n\\ge 1,$ and ${a}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ So, the series is convergent by the alternating series test.\n\nBecause our series is a convergent alternating series, the alternating series error bound applies. In particular, for the $5$th partial sum, it states that\n\n$$\n|S-{s}_{5}|<|{b}_{6}|=|\\frac{{(-2)}^{6}}{(6+1)!}|=\\frac{64}{5040}=\\frac{4}{315}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=\\frac{4}{315}.$"} :step/id #uuid "e0477d66-d0da-57fe-ae64-7a88c2a0d0bc"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540177 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101467 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f76dabc-e965-5bd2-946b-b648a0b45235" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{60}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099964} {:answer/id #uuid "1e5e40ed-6ab4-5198-a8e6-354713c86281" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{120}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099965} {:answer/id #uuid "4261f47f-7b9d-57ec-96d7-399ccbe9f256" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099966} {:answer/id #uuid "6548e5c8-a68d-5619-8a7c-27deb3295f97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{720}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099967} {:answer/id #uuid "708e3bce-0e4d-573c-ae65-e71e364a43b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099968}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186099965} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d626168-faee-5bbe-9336-29515ea5b9d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099963}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cca5f54f-1a2c-5d55-ba21-def4752edd37" :question/math-academy-id "q-50830" :question/problem "Suppose that \n\n$$\nS={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}n}{n!}\n$$\n\nand that ${s}_{5}$ is the fifth partial sum of $S.$ According to the alternating series error bound, $|S-{s}_{5}|<k$ for some real number $k.$ Find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{n}=\\frac{n}{n!}$ and ${b}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n}n}{n!}.$ Then ${a}_{n}$ is positive and decreasing for $n\\ge 1,$ and ${a}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ So, the series is convergent by the alternating series test.\n\nBecause our series is a convergent alternating series, the alternating series error bound applies. In particular, for the $5$th partial sum, it states that\n\n$$\n|S-{s}_{5}|<|{b}_{6}|=|\\frac{(-1)^{6}\\cdot 6}{6!}|=\\frac{6}{720}=\\frac{1}{120}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $k=\\frac{1}{120}.$"} :step/id #uuid "e78a6e2d-2359-5c60-b93c-2d7ced996f4e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540154}}] :activity/title "The Alternating Series Error Bound" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539468}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046673} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575649} :activity/id #uuid "9a0c4459-7e42-515e-ba77-ca28d96e8d50" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050912 :topic/id #uuid "70abc32f-7c95-5eaa-bc37-2779f513c439" :topic/math-academy-id 1174} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575639 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575574 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ccf8156a-5145-53a0-acb6-e6e427f7cdcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575312}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575312} :answer-field/id #uuid "3de1ab9d-bf97-57ce-8fb8-7959134261a6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575445}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8f270d15-6fe5-55df-829e-27dd74957fdc" :question/math-academy-id "q-352774" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\nS={\\sum }_{n=3}^{\\infty }\\frac{{2(-1)}^{n}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2n+1)}\n$$\n\nand that ${s}_{i}$ is the $i$th partial sum of $S,$ use the alternating series error bound to determine an inequality that gives the values of $i$ such that $|S-{s}_{i}|<0.2.$\n\n$\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2i+3)\\ge$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let ${a}_{n}=\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2n+1)}$ and ${b}_{n}=\\frac{{(-1)}^{n}\\,2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2n+1)}.$ Then ${a}_{n}$ is decreasing for $n\\ge 3$ and ${a}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ So, the series is convergent by the alternating series test. \n\nBecause our series is a convergent alternating series, the alternating series error bound applies. In particular, for the $i$th partial sum, it states that\n\n$$\n|S-{s}_{i}|<|{b}_{i+1}|.\n$$\n\nSo, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{b}_{i+1}|=|\\frac{(-1)^{i+1}\\,2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2(i+1)+1)}| & \\le 0.2 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2i+3)} & \\le 0.2 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2i+3)} & \\le \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 10 & \\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2i+3) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2i+3) & \\ge 10.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "212a1ba6-0439-5eb5-9fa6-0549590c783c"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575649 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575557 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19048534-c446-541b-92e4-f87a572f0445" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186574990} {:answer/id #uuid "2986023f-c3b0-5d34-89e7-d5b18cc1b181" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575013} {:answer/id #uuid "660039ee-808b-5909-a6a4-855d47eca687" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{49}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575132} {:answer/id #uuid "c054db7d-8b25-59ff-85a1-fc53976cbd21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{49}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575292} {:answer/id #uuid "f26e9ff2-3fea-5e99-8496-28dd0c86a06d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{49}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575376}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575376} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a59751e-949f-5d36-80c1-fa6f0759ee0d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575420}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6c4bc11a-cdaa-550d-94a0-3ff2e50cf498" :question/math-academy-id "q-15103" :question/problem "Suppose that\n\n$$\nS={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{(-1)}^{n}}{{n}^{2}}\n$$\n\nand that ${s}_{6}$ is the sixth partial sum of $S.$ According to the alternating series error bound, $|S-{s}_{6}|<k$ for some real number $k.$ Find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}$ and ${b}_{n}=\\frac{{(-1)}^{n}}{{n}^{2}}.$ Then ${a}_{n}$ is positive and decreasing for $n\\ge 1,$ and ${a}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ So, the series is convergent by the alternating series test.\n\nBecause our series is a convergent alternating series, the alternating series error bound applies. In particular, for the $6$th partial sum, it states that\n\n$$\n|S-{s}_{6}|<|{b}_{7}|=|\\frac{(-1)^{7}}{{7}^{2}}|=\\frac{1}{49}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $k=\\frac{1}{49}.$"} :step/id #uuid "50136f0e-287a-5a86-9add-5cd78eb80231" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575684}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575652 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575591 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6fbc8956-7af9-5d82-88ed-b5cd5cfd7c9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575152}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575152} :answer-field/id #uuid "fdd473d7-ac4e-56be-8ce8-60ffb0f1e888" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575522}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b6f1577d-5608-56ec-bbab-86d743866f2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-352762" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\nS={\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n+1}}{n({n}^{2}+4)}\n$$\n\nand that ${s}_{i}$ is the $i$th partial sum of $S,$ use the alternating series error bound to determine an inequality that gives the values of $i$ such that\n$|S-{s}_{i}|<0.05.$\n\n${i}^{3}+3{i}^{2}+7i+5\\ge$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{3}{n({n}^{2}+4)}\\,\\text{and}\\,{b}_{n}=\\frac{3(-1)^{n+1}}{n({n}^{2}+4)}.\n$$\n\nThen ${a}_{n}$ is decreasing for $n\\ge 2$ and ${a}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ Therefore, the series is convergent by the alternating series test.\n\nBecause the series is a convergent alternating series, the alternating series error bound applies. In particular, for the $i$th partial sum, it states that\n\n$$\n|S-{s}_{i}|<|{b}_{i+1}|.\n$$\n\nThus, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{b}_{i+1}|=|\\frac{3(-1)^{i+2}}{(i+1)((i+1)^{2}+4)}| & \\le 0.05 \\\\ \\frac{3}{(i+1)((i+1)^{2}+4)} & \\le \\frac{1}{20} \\\\ 60 & \\le (i+1)((i+1)^{2}+4) \\\\ 60 & \\le (i+1)({i}^{2}+2i+1+4) \\\\ 60 & \\le (i+1)({i}^{2}+2i+5) \\\\ 60 & \\le {i}^{3}+3{i}^{2}+7i+5 \\\\ {i}^{3}+3{i}^{2}+7i+5 & \\ge 60.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "53829707-e1e6-5a85-8f73-c25dca5844f9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575667}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575667 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575625 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b170842b-5b2d-5078-a5a6-acb218dd45e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9998" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575266}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575266} :answer-field/id #uuid "3bf1afec-fd1f-50cb-988c-1d344a155a6e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575444}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fd9b72de-d1e8-5e4c-b443-057fdab0329d" :question/math-academy-id "q-352763" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\nS={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{(-1)}^{n+1}}{\\sqrt{n+1}}\n$$\n\nand that ${s}_{i}$ is the $i$th partial sum of $S,$ use the alternating series error bound to determine the values of $i$ such that $|S-{s}_{i}|<0.01.$\n\n$i\\ge$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let ${a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n+1}}$ and ${b}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{\\sqrt{n+1}}.$ Then ${a}_{n}$ is decreasing for $n\\ge 1$ and ${a}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ So, the series is convergent by the alternating series test. \n\nBecause our series is a convergent alternating series, the alternating series error bound applies. In particular, for the $i$th partial sum, it states that\n\n$$\n|S-{s}_{i}|<|{b}_{i+1}|.\n$$\n\nSo, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{(-1)^{i+2}}{\\sqrt{(i+1)+1}}| & \\le 0.01 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{i+2}} & \\le \\frac{1}{100} \\\\ \\sqrt[2]{i+2} & \\ge 100 \\\\ (i+2)^{1/2} & \\ge 100 \\\\ i+2 & \\ge (100)^{2} \\\\ i+2 & \\ge 10000 \\\\ i & \\ge 9998.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "882b7db4-09fc-5e93-b1f6-eb88c65a7948" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575639}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575684 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575573 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc145cd2-9498-5331-8842-8de1cc06800b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7920}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575310}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575310} :answer-field/id #uuid "159b45da-df98-533e-a437-a9180419cd3d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575414}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8f164f90-e76a-54c1-a336-782b45d61bdf" :question/math-academy-id "q-352756" :question/problem "Suppose that\n\n$$\nS={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{(-1)}^{n}\\,n!}{(n+4)!}\n$$\n\nand that ${s}_{6}$ is the sixth partial sum of $S.$ According to the alternating series error bound, $|S-{s}_{6}|<k$ for some real number $k.$ Find the value of $k.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$k=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{n}=\\frac{n!}{(n+4)!}$ and ${b}_{n}=\\frac{{(-1)}^{n}\\,n!}{(n+4)!}.$ Then ${a}_{n}$ is positive and decreasing for $n\\ge 1,$ and ${a}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ So, the series is convergent by the alternating series test.\n\nBecause our series is a convergent alternating series, the alternating series error bound applies. In particular, for the $6$th partial sum, it states that\n\n$$\n|S-{s}_{6}|<|{b}_{7}|=|\\frac{{(-1)}^{7}\\,7!}{(7+4)!}|=\\frac{7!}{11!}=\\frac{1}{8\\cdot 9\\cdot 10\\cdot 11}=\\frac{1}{7920}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=\\frac{1}{7920}.$"} :step/id #uuid "d8a5ca47-6ee4-5751-a8ec-ed05ca31f3fd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575652}}] :activity/title "The Alternating Series Error Bound" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186574944}]}
